      "attack_vectors": [
        {"vector_type": "web_ui", "auth_mode": "credentials", "status": "confirmed",
         "dkim": "sendgrid.net:s=smtpapi",
         "notes": "WORKS without domain authentication via the UI: Email API > Dynamic Templates > create/open a template > Preview > Edit > Settings > 'Test your email'. The form exposes a free 'From Address' (any value) + recipients + 'Send Test Message' (subject is prefixed 'Test - '). You control only the header From: the envelope MAIL FROM stays bounces@sendgrid.net, so SPF/DKIM are NOT aligned and DMARC is NOT bypassed (an enforced p=quarantine/reject catches it - observed: DMARC_POLICY_QUARANTINE, SPF/DKIM not aligned). Even with p=none DMARC is still evaluated: the alignment failure is scored (Rspamd DMARC_POLICY_SOFTFAIL +1.5 at p=none, DMARC_POLICY_QUARANTINE +3 at p=quarantine) and FORGED_SENDER is always raised (From domain != bounces@sendgrid.net). The payoff is REPUTATION only: the mail leaves SendGrid IPs (listed in the victim's SPF, so the From-domain SPF matches the sending IP), but the verdict then depends on the outbound IP reputation (RBL_MAILSPIKE_* seen on some SendGrid IPs) - tested 2026-10-30, Rspamd / Gmail: p=none 1.70/11, spam; p=quarantine 7.30/11, spam + authentication warning; p=reject 9.80/11, not received. Best case: a target that whitelists SendGrid IPs (e.g. because it uses SendGrid itself). Domain Authentication (Settings > Sender Authentication: 3 CNAMEs with Automated Security, or 2 TXT + 1 MX) proves DNS control, not registrar ownership; Single Sender Verification only validates one From address via an email link."},
        {"vector_type": "smtp_server","auth_mode": "api_key",     "status": "blocked", "port": "587/465", "transport": "client_to_server"},
        {"vector_type": "smtp_server","auth_mode": "credentials", "status": "blocked", "port": "587/465", "transport": "client_to_server"}
      ]
